description.narrative:
Micronesian local communities play a leading and integral role in managing coastal and marine resources in cooperation with local government agencies. Community-based adaptation that involves stakeholders throughout Micronesia must be consistent with the traditional community values prominent in Micronesian culture. This approach is vital to the success of the overall ability of the region to adapt to the effects of climate change. Climate risk management across Palau, FSM, and RMI is likely to be most successful if planned and designed with a motivated community. This happens by taking the time to work with local communities and their leaders, forming partnerships with local stakeholders and non-governmental organizations, and involves a planning structure that involves landowners and those with land use rights. When the community most affected by climate change is involved in designing the tools to manage climate risk, the likelihood that adaptation steps will be successfully implemented is increased significantly. In 2010, natural resource managers who support community-based management efforts in Micronesia recognized the need to begin incorporating climate change adaptation into community processes such as protected areas development and fisheries management.
The proposed EDA programme concept was developed in close consultation with the Palau, FSM and RMI NDAs, as well as regional, national and local conservation and environmental NGOs/CSOs.[1] [https://d.docs.live.net/f220863072513578/Desktop/Nataij/MCT%20EDA/GCF%201st%20review/MCT-EDA-CN-CLEAN-25May2021.docx#_ftn1] This included workshops, bilateral discussions and review of draft programme components by participating entities. This proposed EDA programme will build on and upscale successful interventions from other projects in the region including the Micronesia Challenge, the Pacific Ridge to Reef (R2R) Programme and Adaptation Fund project Enhancing the Climate Resilience of vulnerable island communities in Federated States of Micronesia (please see Annex III for a list of past and ongoing projects in the region and opportunities for this programme to leverage and complement these other donor funded projects). The EDA programme will allow for a region-wide approach to building resilience reduce the vulnerability of the natural ecosystems upon which these communities rely to climate change stressors.
In coordination with local focal points, the programme will identify relevant NGOs and local authorities in FSM, Palau and RMI to lead and implement community-based adaptation projects within locally protected areas and receive capacity support and technical training. The programme will then utilize existing adaptation resources and a community-led process to develop and select ecosystems-based adaptation activities. These activities will then be developed into projects through the programme’s Small Grant’s Facility, facilitated by the accredited entity (MCT). Additionally, a knowledge management system will be designed and implemented in order to capture lessons learned from project implementation and to ensure regional cooperation across the three participating countries.
The programme has three components as identified in Table 3 below.
Table 3: Programme Components
[https://formioblob.blob.core.windows.net/formio/uploads/table3-246b4cf0-5873-44da-b035-788810f2c0e5.jpg?st=2021-05-25T16%3A01%3A43Z&amp;se=2022-05-25T17%3A41%3A43Z&amp;sp=r&amp;sv=2018-03-28&amp;sr=b&amp;sig=T8E%2Fki%2F%2FY59st7b%2FAlXKCyW9ftned6UYgMEkwaFIxp8%3D]
Local communities in FSM, Palau and RMI rely heavily on coastal and marine resources, located in protected areas and undeveloped, natural habitat for food, security, to sustain their livelihoods and generate revenue. These resources are subject to continual degradation from climate change stressors, including shifting precipitation patters, sea-level rise, increased typhoon intensity, and ocean warming, acidification and deoxygenation. Communities face key barriers in reducing the negative impacts of climate change on the natural systems upon which they depend: 1) lack of awareness of specific climate change threats and capacity to address these threats; 2) lack of community-level financing to implement localized projects to address these threats; and 3) lack of resources to engage in regional cooperation and knowledge sharing of ecosystem-based adaptation interventions.
This programme aims to address these barriers through three outcomes. First, the programme will increase NGO and local authority capacity and technical capabilities to address relevant climate change threats. Second, the programme will provide a funding mechanism through the Small Grants Facility to implement local adaptation projects within protected areas and natural habitat. Third, the programme will develop a knowledge management system to ensure regional cooperation and capture lessons learned from sub-project implementation. Please see the Theory of Change diagram (Figure 2).
Under Component 2 of the programme, local adaptation projects to be implemented in PAs through the SGF will be identified during programme implementation. The following project types are indicative of project types to be implemented:[2] [https://d.docs.live.net/f220863072513578/Desktop/Nataij/MCT%20EDA/GCF%201st%20review/MCT-EDA-CN-CLEAN-25May2021.docx#_ftn1][3] [https://d.docs.live.net/f220863072513578/Desktop/Nataij/MCT%20EDA/GCF%201st%20review/MCT-EDA-CN-CLEAN-25May2021.docx#_ftn2]
· Restoration and rehabilitation of ecosystems relevant for adaptation (e.g., mangrove restoration, re-vegetation, sea-grass beds, reforestation, coral reef restoration), including restoration/rehabilitation projects identified in existing management plans;
· Fisheries and coastal resources management in PAs and locally managed areas;
· Watershed management through nature-based interventions;
· Alternative livelihoods; and
· PA designation and completion of PA management plans.
Regarding grant size, projects under the SGF are projected to be in the range of USD 10,000 – 50,000 for CSOs, and USD 50,000 – 200,000 for larger and more established NGOs operating in the region. Each of the three participating countries would then implement 10-15 smaller projects and 5-7 larger projects over the lifetime of the EDA programme. These indicative interventions and grant allocations are preliminary and will be further investigated during full proposal development. This will include ensuring projects under the SGF meet GCF’s ESS Category C standard. A preliminary list of activities that will not be allowed under the SGF include the following:
· Conflict with adopted plans and established uses of the target community(ies);
· Substantially affect a rare or endangered species of animal or plant or the habitat of such species;
· Interfere substantially with the movement of any resident or migratory fish or wildlife species;
· Substantially degrade water quality;
· Substantially diminish habitat for fish, wildlife, or plants;
· Displace a large number of people; and
· Convert prime agricultural land to non-agricultural use or impair the agricultural productivity of prime agricultural land.
Under Component 3 of the programme, the knowledge management system will be designed to build on and complement existing country-specific databases. These include the SPREP data portals for Palau, FSM and RMI.[4] [https://d.docs.live.net/f220863072513578/Desktop/Nataij/MCT%20EDA/GCF%201st%20review/MCT-EDA-CN-CLEAN-25May2021.docx#_ftn3] Thus, the programme will ensure that there is no duplication of efforts amongst existing knowledge management platforms utilized by the three countries.
[https://formioblob.blob.core.windows.net/formio/uploads/mctedacnto-fc29882e-f813-477e-a093-e73ccbdc637a.png?st=2021-05-25T16%3A02%3A53Z&amp;se=2022-05-25T17%3A42%3A53Z&amp;sp=r&amp;sv=2018-03-28&amp;sr=b&amp;sig=yRXB59pGKoxcb1AsEO5EPyZ5%2FiWGKnPCv6OYOQ0X%2FG0%3D]
Figure 2: Theory of Change
National regulatory and legal framework
As discussed in Section B.1, FSM, Palau and RMI each have a regulatory mechanism in place for the designation and management of protected areas at a national level (Protected Area Network) and are participants in a regional, terrestrial and marine protected area programme (the Micronesia Challenge). Additionally, each country has in place measures for the designation and management of locally protected areas. The outcomes of the proposed programme, in coordination with local communities, focal points and the accredited entity, will be aligned with the countries’ existing protected area framework, at the regional, national and local levels.
Micronesian Conservation Trust and Partners
Since its founding in 2002, MCT has managed and supported conservation, climate change adaptation and other sustainable development projects throughout the region of Micronesia (including previous and ongoing projects in FSM, Palau and RMI).[5] [https://d.docs.live.net/f220863072513578/Desktop/Nataij/MCT%20EDA/GCF%201st%20review/MCT-EDA-CN-CLEAN-25May2021.docx#_ftn1] As described in its mission statement, MCT builds partnerships throughout the region, raises and manages funds for conservation and adaptation projects, influences local and regional policy and provides conservation and financing expertise.[6] [https://d.docs.live.net/f220863072513578/Desktop/Nataij/MCT%20EDA/GCF%201st%20review/MCT-EDA-CN-CLEAN-25May2021.docx#_ftn2]
MCT is overseen by a board of 10 regional and international experts and boasts a variety of NGO, government and support partners throughout the region. The organization has robust grant-making and capacity-building procedures, which it has institutionalized as organization policy.[7] [https://d.docs.live.net/f220863072513578/Desktop/Nataij/MCT%20EDA/GCF%201st%20review/MCT-EDA-CN-CLEAN-25May2021.docx#_ftn1] As part of its programming, MCT awards and manages subgrants to organizations and communities to enhance and help meet the targets of the Micronesia Challenge. The subgrants cover conservation and climate change adaptation projects such as the Pakin Community Association (Pohnpei, FSM) “Building Pakin Community Resiliency to Climate Change Through an enhanced ecosystem-based approach to Fisheries Management” project. MCT’s partners and its experience and expertise in implementing and managing subgrants throughout Micronesia make it uniquely qualified to undertake the proposed programme.
Implementation arrangements
Overall EDA Programme
This programme will be managed by MCT as a regional Direct Access Entity with the following fiduciary functions: micro-size, project management, category C (E&S) and grants award. MCT will be responsible for the overall programme management. MCT is the financing mechanism for the Micronesia Challenge and is a member of the Micronesia Challenge Steering Committee. MCT additionally houses the Micronesia Challenge Socio-Economic Coordinator and the Micronesia Challenge Terrestrial Monitoring Coordinator who lead the MC core teams that measure the indicators and success. Implementation will include the following representatives:
EDA Programme Coordination Unit (ECU) A dedicated EDA Programme Coordination Unit (ECU) will be hosted at MCT’s headquarters in Pohnpei FSM, with one Programme Coordinator, one Programme Administrative Assistant, and one Grants Officer. The ECU will run the day-to-day operations of the EDA programme and will manage the grant facility (more details below).
NDA for each country: The NDA acts as the official GCF focal point in-country. The agreement signed with the MCT and each government will cover the NDA’s role in the programme, including as appropriate: local recruitment of one staff at each NDA office to serve as a liaison between the ECU and NGOs/local government units; domestic travel; office equipment (laptop etc.). The NDA from each country will work alongside MCT and the ECU to establish the EDA programme’s governing bodies and will also assist in the supervision of sub-grant design and implementation.
Country EDA Focal Points: Each country will recruit a country EDA focal point to support the ECU and NDA to disseminate information about the programme to NGOs and local government officials and to support the local organizing of meetings/training/workshops.
EDA Programme Board (EPB): The Programme Board will be a regional body chaired by the three NDAs and will be responsible for taking corrective action as needed to ensure the EDA programme achieves the desired results. In case consensus cannot be reached within the Board, the MCT Representative (or their designate) will mediate to find consensus and, if this cannot be found, will take the final decision to ensure programme implementation is not unduly delayed. The EPB will meet once a year with the possiblity to hold e-meeitngs for final sub-grant approvals every 6-months or as needed.
Specific responsibilities of the EPB include:
· Provide overall guidance and direction to the programme, ensuring it remains within any specified constraints;
· Address programme issues as raised by the programme coordinator;
· Provide guidance on new programme risks, and agree on possible mitigation and management actions to address specific risks;
· Advise on major and minor amendments to the programme within the parameters set by MCT;
· Ensure coordination between various donor and government-funded projects and programmes;
· Ensure coordination with various government agencies and their participation in programme activities;
· Track and monitor co-financing for this programme;
· Review the programme progress, assess performance, and appraise the Annual Work Plan for the following year;
· Provide direction and recommendations to ensure that the agreed deliverables are produced satisfactorily according to plans;
· Address programme-level grievances;
· Approve the programme Inception Report, Mid-term Review and Terminal Evaluation reports and corresponding management responses;
· Review the final programme report package during an end-of-programme review meeting to discuss lesson learned and opportunities for scaling up
The Board members will include representatives from the following groups:
· NDAs from FSM, Palau, RMI (co-chairs)
· Representative from key government divisions
· Representative of NGOs/CSOs
MCT will serve in the quality assurance role and will support the EPB and ECU by carrying out objective and independent programme oversight and monitoring functions. This role ensures appropriate programme management milestones are managed and completed. The programme assurance role is totally independent of the programme coordination function. Figure 3. below presents an overview of the programme implementation arrangements (this will be further developed and agreed to during full proposal development).
[data:image/png;base64,]
Figure 3. EDA Programme Implementation
Small Grants Facility
In addition to the overall programme structure, the small grants facility (SGF) (Component 2) will have a separate implementation structure to review, screen and approve sub-grant proposals. The proposed structure will be run through MCT’s existing grant mechanism. For the EDA, the ECU will be responsible for putting out the call for proposals to initiate proposal submissions. The concept papers will be screened by the ECU to determine project eligibility. If a project falls within the appropriate thematic areas and there is no duplication with other ongoing support in-country, the EOI will be cleared to the submission to for full proposals.
Once cleared the full submission will be submitted and put through a due diligence screening. This screening will be aligned with the GCF investment criteria. The details for how this will be done will be fully developed through the full project development process and will ensure that EDA grants will only be awarded to projects that meet the following criteria, which will be further developed in the call for proposal as well as listed and explained in the calls for proposals:
· Climate change adaptation impact potential
o Direct and indirect beneficiaries whose vulnerability is reduced or resilience is increased (with detailed information on locations & exposure, gender, social conditions etc.)
o Inclusion of most vulnerable populations (e.g. at risk of being displaced, women, those at the extremes of age, those with pre-existing health problems, the poor and socially disadvantaged, outer islands communities, etc.)
o Climate change resilient jobs/livelihoods created
o Dependency on livelihoods vulnerable to climate change reduced
o Increase in generation and use of climate information in local decision-making
· Transformational/paradigm shift
o Degree to which the proposed project addresses prevailing unsustainable practices/situations towards a resilient development pathway
o Degree to which the project is innovative/business unusual
o Potential for scaling up and replication elsewhere if the project proves successful
o Potential for knowledge and learning
o Sustainability of outcomes and results beyond completion of the intervention (projects will need to include an exit strategy and demonstrate commitments towards long-term sustainability through the use of specific tools such as, for instance, operation & maintenance plans)
· Sustainable development
o Environmental Co-Benefits (not directly climate-related), such as biodiversity, soil quality, air quality etc.
o Social Co-Benefits such as health and safety, access to education, cultural preservation etc.
o Economic Co-Benefits such as expanded and enhanced job markets, job creation and poverty alleviation, improved sector income-generating capacity, increase in agricultural productivity etc.
o Gender-Sensitive Development: degree to which the project addresses prevailing gender inequalities in general and with regards to climate change vulnerability and risks in particular
· Needs from local communities
o Degree to which the proposals account for local communities’ needs.
o Direct community involvement in the project implementation
· Alignment with national or sub-national priorities
o Degree to which the project clearly aligns with national or subnational priorities (e.g. contributing to objectives of national climate change policies, action plans implementation of JSAPs or local DRR plans)
· Efficiency and effectiveness
o Cost-effectiveness and efficiency
o Co-financing
o Financial viability
o Application of best practices
A separate Grants Committee will be formed with technical representatives from each of the three beneficiary countries to make a final recommendation to the EDA Programme Board (EPB) for final approval. Figure 4 below presents an overview of the grant mechanism, which will be further refined and confirmed through stakeholder consultations including gender-specific consultations during the full proposal development (incl. the development of a feasibility study).
[https://formioblob.blob.core.windows.net/formio/uploads/smallgrant-aa5fee3f-cb58-4415-a794-b1480fd6fd11.png?st=2021-05-25T16%3A09%3A14Z&amp;se=2022-05-25T17%3A49%3A14Z&amp;sp=r&amp;sv=2018-03-28&amp;sr=b&amp;sig=Pk%2Be7w%2BAoqH%2BFE6oggVIuxkUx%2F7IIE72BFI77v8lt%2F4%3D]
Figure 4. Small Grants Mechanism
Environmental and social safeguard assessment
All grant proposals will include an environmental and social safeguard screening to avoid, minimize and mitigate any harm to people and ecosystems and to incorporate environmental and social concerns as an intrinsic part of project cycle management. The programme will only approve proposals that are categorized as low risk in line with MCT’s Environmental and Social Safeguard policy and the GCF’s environmental and social safeguards (category C). More details for how the specific screenings and approvals will be undertaken will be determined during full programme development.
Gender Mainstreaming
All grant proposals will include a gender analysis and will provide information on how the sub-grant has integrated gender considerations have been integrated into project design. A gender clearance process will be undertaken to ensure all proposals adequately mainstream and address gender appropriately. Similar to the E&S assessment, more details for how the programme will ensure appropriate gender analysis and action plans are embeded in sub-grant design will be determined during full programme development
Monitoring and Evaluation of SGF Projects
Projects implemented through the SGF will focus on supporting vulnerable communities to identify and implement adaptation response measures within PAs in the three participating countries. Currently, MCT utilizes the Micronesia Protected Area Management Effectiveness tool (MPAME), which is a methodology for assessing management effectiveness of both terrestrial and marine conservation areas in the Micronesia region.[8] [https://d.docs.live.net/f220863072513578/Desktop/Nataij/MCT%20EDA/GCF%201st%20review/MCT-EDA-CN-CLEAN-25May2021.docx#_ftn1] The MPAME provides PA managers, policy makers and other relevant stakeholders a tool to achieve PA goals by enabling a rapid assessment of the overall management effectiveness of a PA or a network of PAs. The tool is Excel-based and assesses effectiveness in 11 management categories: Biophysical assessments, Conservation effect (including Ecosystem services), Enforcement, Finance, Infrastructure and equipment, Legal framework, Planning, Socio-economics assessments, Staffing, Stakeholder engagement and Traditional knowledge. The MPAME will be utilized to determine effective PA management of relevant EDA grants and provide guidance on measures needed to achieve effectiveness. The MPAME is currently deployed within PAs every 2-4 years and findings from the assessments assist in and facilitate adaptive management of PAs.[9] [https://d.docs.live.net/f220863072513578/Desktop/Nataij/MCT%20EDA/GCF%201st%20review/MCT-EDA-CN-CLEAN-25May2021.docx#_ftn2] Additional monitoring and evaluation mechanisms (including the Management Effectiveness Tracking Tool (METT)) will be identified during development of the full programme proposal.
Risks and Mitigation Measures
